SOAPBOX: This was a Multi-operator, Single-transmitter operation with three ops
SOAPBOX: remote from the fabulous mountaintop station on Mount Umunhum.
SOAPBOX: K6UFO had this crazy idea to set a M/S Record for the USA, and
SOAPBOX: maybe we did it. K6TD likes digital operating, and was a wizard at
SOAPBOX: getting our remote connections and logging working to the mountaintop
SOAPBOX: station - with VPNs, internet logbooks, TeamViewer, etc... ND2T is a
SOAPBOX: lively optimist and able to keep us moving forward, finding and solving
SOAPBOX: problems, getting us in practice, and pulling together.
SOAPBOX: Everyone had operated FT8/4 before, and was familiar with the
SOAPBOX: Writelog/Digirite software. Everyone was an experienced contest
SOAPBOX: operator, and knew how to keep butt-in-chair, monitor the rate, and
SOAPBOX: pounce on Mults. No major mistakes were made. Without an SO2R-
SOAPBOX: capable station, we could only operate one-band, one-cycle so we
SOAPBOX: tried to keep busy every transmit cycle, and queue up our next target
SOAPBOX: while receiving. Our average rate was 25, our best hour was a 47,
SOAPBOX: our worst was a 6. But the worst arrived late in the contest when
SOAPBOX: K6TD first switched to 80M at 3AM, called CQ, and ZL3TE called,
SOAPBOX: and was worked without any repeats - two overs! Then the internet
SOAPBOX: failed. Cloudflare blames CenturyLink, etc... but the internet was
SOAPBOX: terrible right through to the end of contest.
SOAPBOX: 20m was the best band all day long, and we spent 11 hours there.
SOAPBOX: ND2T spent an hour to pull the easy Qs and Mults from 15m, but
SOAPBOX: not worth more time. 40m was second best band and delivered for
SOAPBOX: over seven hours. 80m was difficult, but needed at the start and finish,
SOAPBOX: and a good run at 05Z. We never bothered with 160m, nor 10m.
SOAPBOX: Mark K6UFO
SOAPBOX: for  K6TD, ND2T and K6UFO.
SOAPBOX: Flex 6700 and SmartSDR
SOAPBOX: Alpha 9500 running 500 to 800 watts
SOAPBOX: Writelog and Digirite software
SOAPBOX: JK Mid-Tri (3 el on 20m, 4 el on 15m) at 95 and 45 feet high
SOAPBOX: JK 4 el 40m yagi at 110 feet
SOAPBOX: 80m 4-square array
